00:12:40.261 --> 00:12:43.371
I could call it, you know, a short day.

366
00:12:43.371 --> 00:12:45.764
I could say, okay, today we're gonna do 40 miles

367
00:12:45.764 --> 00:12:47.418
instead of 50 or whatever.

368
00:12:47.418 --> 00:12:50.881
And that's just not the case with Run2Boston.

369
00:12:50.881 --> 00:12:53.899
We have to cover 70 miles every day,

370
00:12:53.899 --> 00:12:57.814
and I made a lot of mistakes in 2008.

371
00:12:57.814 --> 00:12:59.271
When you're you and Marshall working that-

372
00:12:59.271 --> 00:13:00.104
Correct.

373
00:13:00.104 --> 00:13:00.937
The run across the US.

374
00:13:00.937 --> 00:13:02.612
Yeah, I just, it was, you know,

375
00:13:02.612 --> 00:13:05.478
one thing is I spent a ridiculous amount of time

376
00:13:05.478 --> 00:13:06.769
before that expedition.

377
00:13:06.769 --> 00:13:08.870
I mean, for six months

378
00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:13.084
having to deal with almost every aspect of the production.

379
00:13:13.084 --> 00:13:13.917
It was a film.

380
00:13:13.917 --> 00:13:15.266
There was, you know,

381
00:13:15.266 --> 00:13:17.481
me dealing with my own training and all of that.

382
00:13:17.481 --> 00:13:19.352
And I really just didn't,

383
00:13:19.352 --> 00:13:21.063
we had a great production company,

384
00:13:21.063 --> 00:13:25.250
Next Productions that was doing the production of the film

385
00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:27.457
and they were awesome,

386
00:13:27.457 --> 00:13:30.907
