00:17:01.055 --> 00:17:02.642
And I'm like, you don't understand like,

478
00:17:02.642 --> 00:17:03.886
there's a lot of walking.

479
00:17:03.886 --> 00:17:06.228
Every time there's an incline, people are walking.

480
00:17:06.228 --> 00:17:07.061
They're marching.

481
00:17:07.061 --> 00:17:09.846
They're doing a fast paced hike or what have you.

482
00:17:09.846 --> 00:17:13.013
So to me, it seems like that would be,

483
00:17:14.208 --> 00:17:17.506
of course, that's the answer to pacing yourself

484
00:17:17.506 --> 00:17:19.337
all the way across the US.

485
00:17:19.337 --> 00:17:20.670
Well, Badwater's a great example

486
00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:23.230
because when I speak to audiences very often,

487
00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:25.550
one of the first questions I'll ask is I'll say,

488
00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:28.479
"Okay, who in here can run an 11 minute mile?"

489
00:17:28.479 --> 00:17:30.719
Most everybody in the room raises their hands.

490
00:17:30.719 --> 00:17:31.552
Yeah.

491
00:17:31.552 --> 00:17:32.538
I'm like going just 1 mile, 11 minutes?

492
00:17:32.538 --> 00:17:34.413
And they're like, everybody's got their hands up.

493
00:17:34.413 --> 00:17:36.827
Like, okay, if you could do that

494
00:17:36.827 --> 00:17:38.686
for every mile at Badwater,

495
00:17:38.686 --> 00:17:40.528
you would set a new course record.

496
00:17:40.528 --> 00:17:41.361
You wouldn't, yeah.
(Rich laughing)

497
00:17:41.361 --> 00:17:42.325
You know you wouldn't just win.

498
00:17:42.325 --> 00:17:43.703
You'd set a new course record,

499
00:17:43.703 --> 00:17:45.253
and go looking around, going what?

500
00:17:45.253 --> 00:17:46.289
I'm like, yeah.

501
00:17:46.289 --> 00:17:48.797
I said, it's also, you know,

502
00:17:48.797 --> 00:17:52.457
it's like running on the sun and it's crazy hot.

503
00:17:52.457 --> 00:17:54.472
And there's other things that just happen

504
00:17:54.472 --> 00:17:56.462
that you can't imagine would happen.

505
00:17:56.462 --> 00:17:58.731
But in Running America,

506
00:17:58.731 --> 00:18:02.003
I mean, Running America back in 2008,

507
00:18:02.003 --> 00:18:05.420
what I learned, I tried to be proactive

508
00:18:05.420 --> 00:18:09.826
and account for every possible thing that could go wrong.

509
00:18:09.826 --> 00:18:13.342
And I think I did that to a certain degree,

510
00:18:13.342 --> 00:18:15.815
but every possible thing did go wrong.

511
00:18:15.815 --> 00:18:17.837
And so while I was,

512
00:18:17.837 --> 00:18:20.961
while I had acknowledged those things might happen,

513
00:18:20.961 --> 00:18:24.314
I really wasn't completely prepared for what to do about it

514
00:18:24.314 --> 00:18:25.147
