2104
01:16:58.643 --> 01:17:01.998
And I have no idea what that even means yet,

2105
01:17:01.998 --> 01:17:05.726
but, you know, ultimately I went in there

2106
01:17:05.726 --> 01:17:07.143
just saying that,

2107
01:17:07.990 --> 01:17:09.627
I can be one of those guys,

2108
01:17:09.627 --> 01:17:12.742
and I saw them every day who had long prison sentences

2109
01:17:12.742 --> 01:17:15.978
and they checked off the calendar every day,

2110
01:17:15.978 --> 01:17:18.600
and to me that would've been maddening.

2111
01:17:18.600 --> 01:17:19.433
I don't know if I would've made it-

2112
01:17:19.433 --> 01:17:20.266
Right.

2113
01:17:20.266 --> 01:17:21.803
Through my short time.

2114
01:17:21.803 --> 01:17:24.097
It was a year and a half, which is a long time,

2115
01:17:24.097 --> 01:17:26.342
but relatively speaking,

2116
01:17:26.342 --> 01:17:28.389
not that long compared to many of them.

2117
01:17:28.389 --> 01:17:30.866
Right, 16 months, but was the sentence longer?

2118
01:17:30.866 --> 01:17:31.699
Sentence was 21 months.

2119
01:17:31.699 --> 01:17:32.759
21 months.

2120
01:17:32.759 --> 01:17:34.084
And the way the federal system works

2121
01:17:34.084 --> 01:17:34.968
is you sort of get a,

2122
01:17:34.968 --> 01:17:38.426
you get a pre nearly 15% discount-

2123
01:17:38.426 --> 01:17:39.440
(Charlie laughing)

2124
01:17:39.440 --> 01:17:40.432
Right.
On your sentence.

2125
01:17:40.432 --> 01:17:41.265
(Rich laughing)

2126
01:17:41.265 --> 01:17:43.854
You get your good behavior in advance,

2127
01:17:43.854 --> 01:17:45.243
and if you screw it up-

2128
01:17:45.243 --> 01:17:46.726
They can ruin it.
They take it away.

2129
01:17:46.726 --> 01:17:47.559
Right.
I got you.

2130
01:17:47.559 --> 01:17:50.438
And then technically you,

2131
01:17:50.438 --> 01:17:51.438
you normally would get-

2132
01:17:51.438 --> 01:17:52.271
So generous of them.

2133
01:17:52.271 --> 01:17:53.132
(Charlie laughing)
Yeah, absolutely.

2134
01:17:53.132 --> 01:17:55.971
You normally would get 10% of the halfway house.

2135
01:17:55.971 --> 01:17:57.147
And so that's what I got to,

2136
01:17:57.147 --> 01:17:58.144
that's why it was a,

2137
01:17:58.144 --> 01:18:00.151
it was a 21 month sentence.

2138
01:18:00.151 --> 01:18:03.353
Basically, I had to serve 18 months of it,

2139
01:18:03.353 --> 01:18:06.005
but after 16 months I was released to a halfway house-

2140
01:18:06.005 --> 01:18:06.838
Gotcha.

2141
01:18:06.838 --> 01:18:08.050
In Greensboro, which actually, by the way,

2142
01:18:08.050 --> 01:18:09.296
it was far worse than prison.

2143
01:18:09.296 --> 01:18:10.643
Oh really?
Yeah.

2144
01:18:10.643 --> 01:18:13.600
Well, in a camp like where I was,

2145
01:18:13.600 --> 01:18:15.512
and basically there with non-violent criminals,

2146
01:18:15.512 --> 01:18:17.545
generally speaking, I mean, a lot of them-

2147
01:18:17.545 --> 01:18:18.934
Well, Beckley was divided.

2148
01:18:18.934 --> 01:18:20.720
There's was a like a minimum-

2149
01:18:20.720 --> 01:18:21.719
Medium, yeah.

2150
01:18:21.719 --> 01:18:22.819
Security and a medium.

2151
01:18:22.819 --> 01:18:23.652
A medium right.

2152
01:18:23.652 --> 01:18:26.611
And the medium, and I got to know some of those guys too,

2153
01:18:26.611 --> 01:18:29.847
'cause they would graduate from there.

2154
01:18:29.847 --> 01:18:31.302
I don't mean graduate-
Right.

2155
01:18:31.302 --> 01:18:32.135
Like high school,

2156
01:18:32.135 --> 01:18:34.976
but, you know, they would move from there if they,

2157
01:18:34.976 --> 01:18:37.325
if they were less than 10 years left on their sentence,

2158
01:18:37.325 --> 01:18:41.796
and whatever, they might come to the lower security place.

2159
01:18:41.796 --> 01:18:43.469
And you know, what I,

2160
01:18:43.469 --> 01:18:46.908
what I found was of course that,

2161
01:18:46.908 --> 01:18:49.344
these were almost all just regular guys.

2162
01:18:49.344 --> 01:18:51.081
But when I got to the halfway house,

2163
01:18:51.081 --> 01:18:53.144
you have guys from all security levels

2164
01:18:53.144 --> 01:18:55.766
that are being dumped into the halfway house.

2165
01:18:55.766 --> 01:18:56.644
So you got some-

2166
01:18:56.644 --> 01:18:57.477
Right.

2167
01:18:57.477 --> 01:18:59.824
Some rapists and murderers and some,

2168
01:18:59.824 --> 01:19:02.113
some genuine bad guys in there

2169
01:19:02.113 --> 01:19:06.595
and you're 30 people stuck in one room on bunk beds

2170
01:19:06.595 --> 01:19:08.262
and you can't leave.

2171
01:19:11.127 --> 01:19:11.960
It was tough.

2172
01:19:11.960 --> 01:19:13.716
How different is it from?

2173
01:19:13.716 --> 01:19:16.189
I mean, my only sort of point of context,

2174
01:19:16.189 --> 01:19:18.576
other than doing the occasional panel,

2175
01:19:18.576 --> 01:19:21.623
in downtown LA is what I see on television

2176
01:19:21.623 --> 01:19:22.827
or what I see in movies.

2177
01:19:22.827 --> 01:19:23.972
This is what,

2178
01:19:23.972 --> 01:19:26.148
this is the way a prison guard acts and behaves.

2179
01:19:26.148 --> 01:19:28.761
It's all informed by media that I've seen,

2180
01:19:28.761 --> 01:19:32.124
it's all crafted by a screenwriter and a director.

2181
01:19:32.124 --> 01:19:33.541
So how, you know,

2182
01:19:34.923 --> 01:19:38.061
how different is the real life experience of being

2183
01:19:38.061 --> 01:19:39.686
in a place like that versus,

2184
01:19:39.686 --> 01:19:42.051
what did you expect and how is it different?

2185
01:19:42.051 --> 01:19:44.269
Well, of course, I feared the worst,

2186
01:19:44.269 --> 01:19:45.460
you know, all the time,

2187
01:19:45.460 --> 01:19:48.130
I went in there on Valentine's day 2011,

2188
01:19:48.130 --> 01:19:49.957
that was the first day of my sentence.

2189
01:19:49.957 --> 01:19:52.537
And, you know, my major concern

2190
01:19:52.537 --> 01:19:54.269
was being someone's Valentine that night.

2191
01:19:54.269 --> 01:19:55.230
Yeah.

2192
01:19:55.230 --> 01:19:58.015
And or any night and-

2193
01:19:58.015 --> 01:19:58.848
Or any morning.

2194
01:19:58.848 --> 01:20:00.026
Or any morning or any time,

2195
01:20:00.026 --> 01:20:01.288
and that,
(Rich laughing)

2196
01:20:01.288 --> 01:20:04.609
you know, that didn't happen at least.

2197
01:20:04.609 --> 01:20:06.141
I'm debating asking you the question of-

2198
01:20:06.141 --> 01:20:06.974
Right.

2199
01:20:06.974 --> 01:20:08.176
You did become somebody's Valentine.

2200
01:20:08.176 --> 01:20:09.009
(Charlie laughing)

2201
01:20:09.009 --> 01:20:09.842
Well, I'll never tell.

2202
01:20:09.842 --> 01:20:10.675
(Rich laughing)

2203
01:20:10.675 --> 01:20:11.508
Yeah.

2204
01:20:11.508 --> 01:20:12.341
We've been sworn to see you after.

2205
01:20:12.341 --> 01:20:13.225
Maybe that'll be in the book,

2206
01:20:13.225 --> 01:20:14.709
You'll have to read the book, exactly.

2207
01:20:14.709 --> 01:20:15.542
(Rich laughing)

2208
01:20:15.542 --> 01:20:19.040
So, but it was, you know, it was very similar

2209
01:20:19.040 --> 01:20:21.753
in many ways to what we do see on television,

2210
01:20:21.753 --> 01:20:24.362
that the difference is I, of course,

2211
01:20:24.362 --> 01:20:25.825
being on that side of it,

2212
01:20:25.825 --> 01:20:27.914
got to see the human aspect of it.

2213
01:20:27.914 --> 01:20:29.497
I got to know guys

2214
01:20:29.497 --> 01:20:32.994
and I got to know their families in visitation.

2215
01:20:32.994 --> 01:20:36.269
I got to see their pictures and hear their stories

2216
01:20:36.269 --> 01:20:38.062
and hear about their lives.

2217
01:20:38.062 --> 01:20:42.340
And it's a funny thing about running and working out,

2218
01:20:42.340 --> 01:20:43.806
all of us who do that,

2219
01:20:43.806 --> 01:20:46.550
understand the comradery that's built.

2220
01:20:46.550 --> 01:20:48.894
And so every day out in the rec yard,

2221
01:20:48.894 --> 01:20:52.548
when we're doing whatever workout we might be doing

2222
01:20:52.548 --> 01:20:57.206
or running around that quarter mile loop and talking

2223
01:20:57.206 --> 01:20:59.482
and just commiserating,

2224
01:20:59.482 --> 01:21:01.649
it was fascinating to find

2225
01:21:03.296 --> 01:21:07.174
the similarities were far greater than the differences

2226
01:21:07.174 --> 01:21:08.603
and it is true.

2227
01:21:08.603 --> 01:21:09.814
Most of them,

2228
01:21:09.814 --> 01:21:11.275
the large majority of people in there

2229
01:21:11.275 --> 01:21:13.442
came from poor backgrounds

2230
01:21:14.423 --> 01:21:17.179
and they were destined for prison.

2231
01:21:17.179 --> 01:21:18.938
I watched this,

2232
01:21:18.938 --> 01:21:21.910
I watched this amazing documentary just this past week

2233
01:21:21.910 --> 01:21:23.494
called "The House I Live In."

2234
01:21:23.494 --> 01:21:25.636
It's on Netflix, by the way.

2235
01:21:25.636 --> 01:21:26.945
They didn't pay me to say that.

2236
01:21:26.945 --> 01:21:28.338
But it was so fascinating

2237
01:21:28.338 --> 01:21:30.872
because it really explained so much around

2238
01:21:30.872 --> 01:21:33.107
the history of incarceration in this country

2239
01:21:33.107 --> 01:21:36.440
and where, you know, where it came from,

2240
01:21:37.358 --> 01:21:41.025
and why we have 14,000 statutes on the books

2241
01:21:42.416 --> 01:21:45.011
that are actually punishable by prison sentences.

2242
01:21:45.011 --> 01:21:48.531
I mean, it's amazing the things that are illegal

2243
01:21:48.531 --> 01:21:52.531
and yet 97% of the people who are in prison

2244
01:21:54.205 --> 01:21:56.915
are there for non-violent crimes.

2245
01:21:56.915 --> 01:22:00.029
So what we do is we lock up the people that we're mad at,

2246
01:22:00.029 --> 01:22:02.293
not the people that we're afraid of.

2247
01:22:02.293 --> 01:22:04.188
And what is the percentage

2248
01:22:04.188 --> 01:22:08.049
of incarcerated people that's related to substance abuse?

2249
01:22:08.049 --> 01:22:08.882
Almost all.

2250
01:22:08.882 --> 01:22:09.715
It's gotta be, yeah.

2251
01:22:09.715 --> 01:22:10.548
Almost all.

2252
01:22:10.548 --> 01:22:12.765
Over 90% are related to drugs.

2253
01:22:12.765 --> 01:22:15.265
And less than 1% of those

2254
01:22:16.627 --> 01:22:20.014
have anything to do with the word, kingpin.

2255
01:22:20.014 --> 01:22:20.874
Right.

2256
01:22:20.874 --> 01:22:24.540
Almost all of them are just regular drug users.

2257
01:22:24.540 --> 01:22:27.992
And yet the lack of treatment

2258
01:22:27.992 --> 01:22:30.459
and rehabilitation within the system.

2259
01:22:30.459 --> 01:22:31.461
It's almost nonexistent.

2260
01:22:31.461 --> 01:22:32.294
It's shocking.

2261
01:22:32.294 --> 01:22:33.199
I mean, we, in this country,

2262
01:22:33.199 --> 01:22:36.204
we have 5% of the population of the world

2263
01:22:36.204 --> 01:22:37.374
in the United States.

2264
01:22:37.374 --> 01:22:42.180
Yet we have 25% of the incarceration in the world.

2265
01:22:42.180 --> 01:22:44.310
So, you know, in the free,

2266
01:22:44.310 --> 01:22:45.606
I don't know what the answer is,

2267
01:22:45.606 --> 01:22:47.787
but I know that we haven't found it.

2268
01:22:47.787 --> 01:22:49.204
And the tough on,

2269
01:22:50.066 --> 01:22:52.508
tough on crime is the words,

2270
01:22:52.508 --> 01:22:55.850
but it's really tough on drugs is,

2271
01:22:55.850 --> 01:22:57.344
is the way it plays out.

2272
01:22:57.344 --> 01:23:00.564
It just really means that alcohol

2273
01:23:00.564 --> 01:23:04.017
and other drugs have just had better lobbyists.

2274
01:23:04.017 --> 01:23:04.850
I mean-

2275
01:23:04.850 --> 01:23:09.358
Well, in the more privatized incarceration becomes,

2276
01:23:09.358 --> 01:23:13.046
then the K street lobbyists start lining their pockets.

2277
01:23:13.046 --> 01:23:15.374
And this is not going in any other direction

2278
01:23:15.374 --> 01:23:17.381
other than to continue to expand.

2279
01:23:17.381 --> 01:23:18.396
Well, if you build it,

2280
01:23:18.396 --> 01:23:20.613
they will fill it, you know, is how it goes.

2281
01:23:20.613 --> 01:23:21.687
And here's the thing.

2282
01:23:21.687 --> 01:23:23.555
I'm certainly a liberal at heart.

2283
01:23:23.555 --> 01:23:24.388
There's no doubt.

2284
01:23:24.388 --> 01:23:26.977
I'm a, you know, I'm a liberal guy,

2285
01:23:26.977 --> 01:23:30.156
but from a fiscal standpoint,

2286
01:23:30.156 --> 01:23:33.648
no matter which side of the political aisle you're on,

2287
01:23:33.648 --> 01:23:37.300
if you take a look at the economics of locking people up

2288
01:23:37.300 --> 01:23:39.043
for incredibly long,

2289
01:23:39.043 --> 01:23:44.043
you lock up a 20 year old for 20 years on a drug sentence,

2290
01:23:44.135 --> 01:23:47.974
that person will end up being a ward of the state

2291
01:23:47.974 --> 01:23:50.994
or ward of the taxpayers for the rest of their lives.

2292
01:23:50.994 --> 01:23:52.169
Right, and what is it?

2293
01:23:52.169 --> 01:23:54.121
Like $60,000 a year or something like that-

2294
01:23:54.121 --> 01:23:55.335
It's crazy.
Per person.

2295
01:23:55.335 --> 01:23:56.482
I'm a tax payer.
And that's maybe,

2296
01:23:56.482 --> 01:23:59.156
and that's only the hard cost maybe of what it,

2297
01:23:59.156 --> 01:24:00.608
feeding them, housing.

2298
01:24:00.608 --> 01:24:01.820
That doesn't count,

2299
01:24:01.820 --> 01:24:03.626
that doesn't actually take into account,

2300
01:24:03.626 --> 01:24:07.069
the cost of maintaining the entire system.

2301
01:24:07.069 --> 01:24:10.469
I mean, it's a crazy, crazy big number.

2302
01:24:10.469 --> 01:24:11.302
Right.

2303
01:24:11.302 --> 01:24:12.906
And when we were,

2304
01:24:12.906 --> 01:24:14.122
although I don't know when this was,

2305
01:24:14.122 --> 01:24:15.889
when we were flush with cash

2306
01:24:15.889 --> 01:24:17.851
or felt like we were in this country,

2307
01:24:17.851 --> 01:24:20.109
then that was fine.

2308
01:24:20.109 --> 01:24:22.051
Everybody gets behind tough on crime.

2309
01:24:22.051 --> 01:24:26.170
You don't want to be the politician that stands up and says,

2310
01:24:26.170 --> 01:24:28.220
we should ease up on these guys some,

2311
01:24:28.220 --> 01:24:31.297
but what it's done is create this situation

2312
01:24:31.297 --> 01:24:32.909
where now we have to pay for it.

2313
01:24:32.909 --> 01:24:35.529
And it's not just paying for it while they're in prison,

2314
01:24:35.529 --> 01:24:37.244
it's paying when they get out.

2315
01:24:37.244 --> 01:24:39.912
And I don't know, as your neighbor,

2316
01:24:39.912 --> 01:24:42.152
would you rather have the guy who,

2317
01:24:42.152 --> 01:24:44.634
who's actually had some strong rehabilitation

2318
01:24:44.634 --> 01:24:46.174
and learned a skill

2319
01:24:46.174 --> 01:24:49.987
and had some positive things happen as your neighbor,

2320
01:24:49.987 --> 01:24:50.829
'cause they're gonna get out

2321
01:24:50.829 --> 01:24:51.971
and they're gonna move somewhere.

2322
01:24:51.971 --> 01:24:55.770
Or would you rather have the angry guy who just lost

2323
01:24:55.770 --> 01:24:59.626
20 years of his life and feels like he was wronged

2324
01:24:59.626 --> 01:25:02.950
and he's just wait, basically, he's just waiting to go back.

2325
01:25:02.950 --> 01:25:03.783
Right.
I mean-

2326
01:25:03.783 --> 01:25:05.139
Well, and he can't get a job

2327
01:25:05.139 --> 01:25:08.016
and he's dependent upon the state to provide.

2328
01:25:08.016 --> 01:25:08.849
Yeah.

2329
01:25:08.849 --> 01:25:11.463
Recidivism rates are 70%.

2330
01:25:11.463 --> 01:25:13.410
I mean, that's why most guys go back.

2331
01:25:13.410 --> 01:25:14.616
They can't-
Right.

2332
01:25:14.616 --> 01:25:17.829
They just can't quite get over the hump.

2333
01:25:17.829 --> 01:25:19.437
And it's not even to say, I mean,

2334
01:25:19.437 --> 01:25:21.163
there's programs to help guys.

2335
01:25:21.163 --> 01:25:22.368
There's all those things,

2336
01:25:22.368 --> 01:25:23.729
but it's easier said than done.

2337
01:25:23.729 --> 01:25:25.969
I mean, for me, I get to check that box.

2338
01:25:25.969 --> 01:25:27.335
Right.
(Charlie laughing)

2339
01:25:27.335 --> 01:25:30.192
For the rest of my life.

2340
01:25:30.192 --> 01:25:32.410
The article that I enjoyed the most on,

2341
01:25:32.410 --> 01:25:35.323
all this sort of articles that are out there from

2342
01:25:35.323 --> 01:25:37.730
Outside, Men's Fitness, New York Times,

2343
01:25:37.730 --> 01:25:39.047
all that kind of stuff that,

2344
01:25:39.047 --> 01:25:41.817
that kind of talks about your prison experience

2345
01:25:41.817 --> 01:25:44.296
was the one in the Oxford American

2346
01:25:44.296 --> 01:25:45.937
'cause that really got at the heart of like,

2347
01:25:45.937 --> 01:25:47.675
this is what it's really like.

2348
01:25:47.675 --> 01:25:49.695
I'll put a link in the show notes up to that,

2349
01:25:49.695 --> 01:25:52.635
but it was a very touching piece.

2350
01:25:52.635 --> 01:25:53.806
Leslie Jamison wrote that,

2351
01:25:53.806 --> 01:25:56.307
and she was incredible.

2352
01:25:56.307 --> 01:25:57.813
She came to visit me,

2353
01:25:57.813 --> 01:26:00.544
and what's funny is it goes back to running.

2354
01:26:00.544 --> 01:26:01.568
Right?
Right.

2355
01:26:01.568 --> 01:26:02.707
'Cause you had met her at an ultra, right?

2356
01:26:02.707 --> 01:26:04.173
I met her at the Barkley.

2357
01:26:04.173 --> 01:26:05.103
I met her at the Barkley,

2358
01:26:05.103 --> 01:26:06.155
and she wasn't running,

2359
01:26:06.155 --> 01:26:09.610
but her brother was actually running the race

2360
01:26:09.610 --> 01:26:13.150
and she wrote a piece about him for the race.

2361
01:26:13.150 --> 01:26:17.915
I wrote a piece about the race for Runner's World actually,

2362
01:26:17.915 --> 01:26:19.293
that got put off being published.

2363
01:26:19.293 --> 01:26:21.688
It actually was published in Runner's World

2364
01:26:21.688 --> 01:26:23.544
while I was at Beckley,
(Rich laughing)

2365
01:26:23.544 --> 01:26:25.880
which was pretty fascinating.

2366
01:26:25.880 --> 01:26:27.763
Do publications like Runner's World,

2367
01:26:27.763 --> 01:26:29.127
are they scared of you now?

2368
01:26:29.127 --> 01:26:30.158
Or they-
No.

2369
